A||||10||loafer87gt||Performance Engine Rebuild ||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||07-19-2000||04:55 PM||||Hey guys, I'm in the process of getting a 3.0 V6 built up for me. So far, the engine is looking as follows, 3.0 V6, performance cam, and 1.6 roller rockers, enlarged throttle body, and air induction system. I am trying to decide if it is worth it or not to get the engine balanced and the heds ported. With the engine balancing, the redline is raised to 7700 RPM, but I would need a chip to get past the 6200 cutoff. I think that this would be real sweet, but I would need to port and polish my heads to get it to breath at the higher RPM's. What sort of HP gains are you guys gaining by porting your heads? I am looking at getting the stage 3 head package from RS motorsports. BTW, does anyone know if Joe Wynams(?) chip removes the rev limiter?||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||07-19-2000||09:36 PM||||Blah, Blah, Blah I screwed up.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||07-19-2000||09:36 PM||||There is no way that motor is going to rev up to seventy-seven grand with the stock reciprocating assembly and stay together even with the engine balancing. There is no way that tuned port injection is going to make any power above 6500 RPM because it is designed for low rpm use with small long runners. You would have to have a real radical camshaft in that engine with the proper valve train to get it to make power to 6500 rpm. The maximum rpm a 2.8 can safely rev up to is 7000rpm with the stock rods. <BR>About the ported heads on a 2.8, I took the flow numbers from Norris racing technologies <A HREF="http://www.norrisracingtech.com/home.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.norrisracingtech.com/home.html</A>  ported heads and plugged them into my Desktop Dyno program and got seven more peak horse power on a 2.8 with a Crane Compucam and ported fiero manifolds. The thing is though it increases power throughout the rpm range, which is the most important thing, not peak power. Balancing the engine should always be done on a performance rebuild no matter what.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||07-20-2000||11:48 PM||||7700 RPM, unlikely. Well, you could get it up there, but it won't like it. <P>I accidentally pegged my X-11 W engine (roller bunch-o-stuff) and I was thankful it stayed together. It didn't sounds nice at all. Consider 6000-6500 a practical limit. When I compared the Z carb engine to the W FI engine, the short runners of the carb engine made it wind better above about 5000, but it would weeze out above 6500.<P>The 3.4L DOHC is a different story. I have Diacom runs of one at 7850 RPM. It will clear 8000. I certainly don't recommend it though.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||07-21-2000||11:28 AM||||That 3.0 aint no Wankle. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> If you want to hit those types of RPM without scattering your motor, try a rotory engine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||3400Fiero||07-21-2000||12:46 PM||||The 3.4L DOHC is a different story. I have Diacom runs of one at 7850 RPM. It will clear 8000. I certainly don't recommend it though.<P>Terry[/B][/QUOTE]<P>How did you get the 3400 up to 7850 RPM?  Clearly not with the stock ECM!  Any info on mods/performance stuff for this engine would be appreciated!<P>Thanks<P>||||reg||5
Z||000006||terryk||07-21-2000||02:11 PM||||I patched around the revlimiter in the code.||||reg||6
Z||000007||Sillhouette.com||07-21-2000||06:00 PM||||Something else to remember it is worth your while if you also get forged pistons.  That will go a long way for further mods like turbo supercharger or NOS.  <BR>Opening the intake and exhaust will definetly give you High end power but will rob some of it away at low end.  You have to play with that.  <BR>The RSM engine revs much easier than the stock ones thanks to the roller rockers and the balanced bottom end.  I found that while passing an intrigue that was doing 220 KPH the Fiero was running at around 5000 RPM still pulling in fifth.  I only got the engine screeming at 6000 rpms in lower gears.  It does not help though since most of the power for the engine comes in at around 3000 - 4500 rpm.  <P>The 3.4 DOHC engine red lines at 7000 rpm from factory.  Don't mistake this engine with the 3400 pushrod one.  The DOHC has four cams and a thick timing belt that is why it can spin that fast.  The 3400 is a pushrod engine and will not spin at 7000 rpm. <P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000008||3400Fiero||07-21-2000||10:55 PM||||My 3400 DOHC from a 1995 Monte Carlo is rev limited at ~6200 RPM!  Did they lower the rev limit in the later years?||||reg||9
Z||000009||terryk||07-22-2000||12:10 AM||||Do you have a 6401 ECM?||||reg||1
Z||000010||olympic||07-22-2000||12:18 AM||||  The 3.4 TDC equipped with an automatic tranny is rev limited alot lower than one with a 5 speed.  The 5 speed ECU would allow around 7200 rpm so if yours is an auto, 6200 rpm is not unreasonable.<P>------------------<BR>....but Officer,  I wasn't speeding.  I was qualifying!||||reg||1
